  "question": {
    "text": "The volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> of an enclosure contains a mixture of three gases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of oxygen,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>28</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>&nbsp;of nitrogen and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>44</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of carbon dioxide at absolute temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math>. Consider <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> as universal gas constant. The pressure of the mixture of gases is :"
